Note `findById` does not return null on miss — it throws `ObjectRetrievalFailureException` / `EmptyResultDataAccessException`, which is translated via the shared helper `Lookups.findOrNotFound(...)` at `/home/daniel/.../src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/common/Lookups.java`.

## 4. OwnerResponse / OwnerRequest DTO classes

There are no classes literally named `OwnerResponse` or `OwnerRequest`. The DTOs are **generated from the OpenAPI contract** (they are not present in `src`; they materialize into `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto` at build time). The relevant generated types are:
- `OwnerFieldsDto` — the request body for POST/PUT (editable fields)
- `OwnerDto` — the response body (extends the fields with `id` and `pets`)
- `OwnerPageDto` — a page wrapper

They are defined in the OpenAPI spec:
**Path:** `/home/daniel/.../src/main/resources/openapi.yml` (schemas start at line 1991)

```yaml
    OwnerFields:
      title: Owner fields
      description: Editable fields of a pet owner.
      type: object
      properties:
        firstName:
          title: First name
          description: The first name of the pet owner.
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 30
          pattern: "^[\\p{L}]+([ '-][\\p{L}]+){0,2}$"
          example: George
        lastName:
          title: Last name
          description: The last name of the pet owner.
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 30
          pattern: "^[\\p{L}]+([ '-][\\p{L}]+){0,2}\\.?$"
          example: Franklin
        address:
          title: Address
          description: The postal address of the pet owner.
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 255
          example: '110 W. Liberty St.'
        city:
          title: City
          description: The city of the pet owner.
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 80
          example: Madison
        telephone:
          title: Telephone number
          description: The telephone number of the pet owner.
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 20
          pattern: '^[0-9]*$'
          example: '6085551023'
      required:
        - firstName
        - lastName
        - address
        - city
        - telephone
    Owner:
      title: Owner
      description: A pet owner.
      allOf:
        - $ref: '#/components/schemas/OwnerFields'
        - type: object
          properties:
            id:
              title: ID
              ...
              readOnly: true
            pets:
              title: Pets
              ...
              readOnly: true
          required:
            - pets
```

The POST operation (`openapi.yml` line ~77) references `$ref: '#/components/schemas/OwnerFields'` as its request body, and line ~281 references it again (the PUT). The Bean Validation annotations (`@NotNull`, `@Size`, `@Pattern`) come out of these schema constraints on the generated `OwnerFieldsDto`, which is what `BuildOwner`'s `@Valid` enforces.

## 5. Escalation handler YAMLs and exception structure

There are no owner-specific escalation YAMLs. Escalations are global, keyed by exception type, under `src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/`:

**`org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.NotFoundException.yml`**
```yaml
handle:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.NotFoundExceptionHandler
```

**`org.springframework.dao.DataIntegrityViolationException.yml`**
```yaml
handle:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.DataIntegrityViolationExceptionHandler
```

**`org.springframework.web.bind.MethodArgumentNotValidException.yml`**
```yaml
handle:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.ValidationExceptionHandler
```

**`java.lang.Exception.yml`**
```yaml
handle:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.GeneralExceptionHandler
```

**`org.springframework.security.authorization.AuthorizationDeniedException.yml`**
```yaml
handle:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.ForbiddenExceptionHandler
```

### Exception structure — there is NO `OwnerNotFoundException`
"Not found" is a single generic exception used across all entities:

**`NotFoundException`** — `/home/daniel/.../src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/NotFoundException.java`
```java
package org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation;

/**
 * Thrown by Load/Build functions when a requested entity does not exist.
 * Handled globally by {@link NotFoundExceptionHandler}, which responds 404 with an empty body.
 */
public class NotFoundException extends Exception {

    public NotFoundException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}
```

**`NotFoundExceptionHandler`** — same package
```java
public class NotFoundExceptionHandler {

    public void handle(@Parameter NotFoundException ex, ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<Void>> response) {
        response.send(ResponseEntity.notFound().build());
    }
}
```
Responds 404 with an empty body.

It is thrown for owners via `LoadOwner` (`/home/daniel/.../rest/function/owner/LoadOwner.java`), which delegates to `Lookups.findOrNotFound(() -> ownerRepository.findById(ownerId), "Owner not found: " + ownerId)`.

**`ValidationExceptionHandler`** builds a `ProblemDetail` (400 Bad Request) with `title` = exception simple name and a `schemaValidationErrors` array of field-level errors. This is what the POST returns when `@Valid` fails.

**`DataIntegrityViolationExceptionHandler`** builds a `ProblemDetail` (NOTE: it returns `HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND`, not 409/422) for data constraint violations.

**`ProblemDetails`** helper — `/home/daniel/.../rest/escalation/ProblemDetails.java` — builds the common ProblemDetail body (type `about:blank`, `title` = `ex.getClass().getSimpleName()`, `detail`, `timestamp`, and empty `schemaValidationErrors`).

Other handlers present: `GeneralExceptionHandler`, `ForbiddenExceptionHandler` (paths in the same escalation package).

## 6. Tests related to creating owners

**Path:** `/home/daniel/.../src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1Tests.java`

This is a `@SpringBootTest @AutoConfigureMockMvc @Transactional` integration test driving the real app through MockMvc against real repositories. The owner-creation tests:

`createOwnerSuccess()` (lines 102-113):
```java
@Test
@WithMockUser(roles = "OWNER_ADMIN")
void createOwnerSuccess() throws Exception {
    String body = """
        {"firstName":"George","lastName":"Franklin","address":"110 W. Liberty St.","city":"Madison","telephone":"6085551023"}
        """;
    mvc.perform(post("/api/owners").content(body)
            .accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON).contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON))
        .andExpect(status().isCreated())
        .andExpect(header().string("Location", org.hamcrest.Matchers.containsString("/api/owners/")))
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.firstName").value("George"));
}
```

`createOwnerValidationError()` (lines 115-126) — omits `firstName`, expects 400 with `$.title` = `MethodArgumentNotValidException` and `$.schemaValidationErrors[0].field` existing:
```java
@Test
@WithMockUser(roles = "OWNER_ADMIN")
void createOwnerValidationError() throws Exception {
    String body = """
        {"lastName":"Franklin","address":"110 W. Liberty St.","city":"Madison","telephone":"6085551023"}
        """;
    mvc.perform(post("/api/owners").content(body)
            .accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON).contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON))
        .andExpect(status().isBadRequest())
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.title").value("MethodArgumentNotValidException"))
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.schemaValidationErrors[0].field").exists());
}
```

The `newOwner(...)` helper (lines 49-58) shows the canonical valid owner values used across tests (firstName "George", address "110 W. Liberty St.", city "Madison", telephone "6085551023"). The file also contains update/delete/get owner tests and nested pet/visit creation tests.

Other test files that mention owners but are not owner-creation unit tests: `src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java`, `AuditLogCapture.java`, `Cp01Tests.java`, plus a JMeter plan and a Postman collection under `src/test/jmeter/` and `src/test/postman/`.

---

### Summary of the POST /api/owners flow
1. Request body JSON is bound to generated `OwnerFieldsDto` and `@Valid`-checked in `BuildOwner`; validation failure -> `MethodArgumentNotValidException` -> `ValidationExceptionHandler` -> 400 ProblemDetail.
2. `BuildOwner` maps `OwnerFieldsDto` -> `Owner` via `OwnerMapper.toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` (id & pets ignored), emits `Out<Owner>`.
3. `SaveOwner` persists via `OwnerRepository.save(owner)` within a transaction.
4. `RespondWithOwnerCreated` maps to `OwnerDto` and returns 201 with `Location` header.

Nothing was modified.

